## The Universal Notebook: What Every Platform Would Need

After mapping all 7 platforms, here's what every single one would have if they built a "notebook":

| # | Field | Why It's Universal |
|---|---|---|
| **1** | **ID** | Every system needs a unique identifier. Immutable. How the system references it internally. |
| **2** | **Name** | Every system needs a human-readable label. "ES — Index macro", "BTC Momentum", "My Workflow". |
| **3** | **Owner** | Every notebook belongs to someone. Even in single-user systems, you need to know who created it for data integrity and future sharing. |
| **4** | **CreatedAt** | Every notebook has a birth moment. Essential for sorting, audit trails, "3 months of research" context. |
| **5** | **UpdatedAt** | Every notebook has a last-touched moment. Essential for recency sorting, "updated 2h ago" in the sidebar. |
| **6** | **Description** | Optional longer context. Not always required, but universally useful. "This notebook tracks macro positioning in ES ahead of FOMC." |
| **7** | **Status** | Every notebook has a lifecycle. Active, archived, deleted. Keeps the workspace clean without losing old work. |
| **8** | **DeletedAt** | Soft delete. Research is precious. You don't lose weeks of work by accident. Enables "recently deleted" recovery. |
| **9** | **Tags** | Categorization. "macro", "crypto", "earnings", "backtest". Every system has some way to label and filter. |
| **10** | **Visibility** | Access control. Public, private, shared. Even in single-user, you need to know if this is draft or published. |

